    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>For Honor has no pity system, but offers direct cosmetic purchases as the primary monetization method.</strong> Loot boxes are a secondary option—players prefer buying specific cosmetics directly from the store rather than gambling. This design philosophy gives players agency over spending rather than forcing gambling.</p><p>The lack of pity system is mitigated by the availability of guaranteed cosmetic purchases. If a player wants a specific skin, they can buy it directly for a fixed price rather than gambling on loot boxes.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2017:</strong> For Honor launches with cosmetic loot boxes as the primary monetization. Initial implementation is controversial but relatively transparent.</p><p><strong>2018-2019:</strong> Direct cosmetic purchases are introduced alongside loot boxes, giving players guaranteed purchase options. This significantly improves monetization ethics.</p><p><strong>2020-2024:</strong> For Honor maintains the cosmetic-only monetization with both loot boxes and direct purchases available. The game is praised for respecting player choice in spending.</p>`,
