    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Buy cosmetics directly from the store instead of gambling on loot boxes. Direct purchases are guaranteed and provide better value.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> For Honor respects free-to-play players—you'll never be disadvantaged by refusing to spend cosmetics.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Set a monthly cosmetic budget (e.g., $5-10) and purchase cosmetics you'll genuinely use consistently.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Seasonal cosmetics rotate—patience sometimes yields cosmetics returning to the store without needing to spend.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Focus on hero cosmetics for characters you main. Cosmetics for heroes you rarely play provide no value.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>For Honor's monetization is significantly improved by offering direct cosmetic purchases alongside loot boxes. This gives players genuine choice—gamble for potential value or guarantee your cosmetic with direct purchase. This is the model that respects player agency while still monetizing cosmetics.</p><p>The cosmetic-only philosophy combined with competitive integrity (no pay-to-win) makes For Honor one of the fairest premium live service games. Free-to-play players are never mechanically disadvantaged, and spending is purely optional.</p><p>For Honor is safe to play and spend on cosmetics. The game respects player time and money through cosmetic-only monetization and direct purchase options. This should be the standard for how live service games implement cosmetic spending.</p>`,
