    overview_html: `<p><strong>World of Tanks</strong> features premium tank boxes costing 9.99 USD per pull, containing random premium tank cosmetics and occasionally gameplay-affecting premium tanks. The system is semi-pay-to-win—while cosmetics are purely cosmetic, rare premium tanks obtained through boxes provide advantages like better earnings and accelerated progression compared to free tanks.</p><p>The monetization is problematic because loot boxes sometimes contain premium tanks (which are progression items), not just cosmetics. Players cannot distinguish between cosmetic boxes and progression boxes without research, making the system deliberately confusing. This creates pay-to-progress mechanics that undermine free-to-play fairness.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>World of Tanks has no pity system and deliberately obscures which boxes contain cosmetics versus progression items.</strong> Players have no guarantee of obtaining specific tanks or cosmetics, and the mixing of progression and cosmetic items makes the system fundamentally unclear.</p><p>The lack of transparency is intentional—the system is designed to keep players uncertain about what they're purchasing, encouraging repeat spending hoping for progression items.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2010:</strong> World of Tanks launches with minimal randomization-based monetization. Early system focuses on premium currency purchases for tanks.</p><p><strong>2015-2019:</strong> Loot boxes are gradually introduced with increasing complexity. Premium tanks begin appearing in randomized boxes, creating pay-to-progress mechanics.</p><p><strong>2020-2024:</strong> World of Tanks continues aggressive loot box monetization with premium tanks in randomized boxes. The system becomes increasingly predatory despite regulatory pressure.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>World of Tanks is notorious for semi-pay-to-win mechanics where premium tanks obtained through loot boxes provide gameplay advantages. The system deliberately mixes cosmetics and progression items, making it impossible for players to determine whether they're purchasing cosmetics or power progression.</p><p>The game has been investigated in multiple regions for predatory mechanics targeting console players (War Gaming also publishes World of Tanks Blitz for consoles). The lack of transparency about which boxes contain progression items is particularly problematic.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Research community databases to identify which loot boxes contain premium tanks versus pure cosmetics. Make informed spending decisions.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> Never open progression-related boxes expecting specific tanks. The system is randomized with no pity.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Accept that premium tank players have gameplay advantages through spending. Focus on skill development instead of expecting tank equality.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Consider whether World of Tanks' pay-to-progress monetization is worth your time. Alternative tank games offer more transparent progression.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Demand transparent odds and pity systems from War Gaming. Regulatory pressure has failed—consumer feedback remains necessary.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>World of Tanks represents a middle-ground between cosmetic and pay-to-win monetization—the worst kind of hybrid system. Premium tanks obtained through loot boxes provide gameplay advantages, but the system deliberately obscures which boxes contain progression items. This confusion is intentional, designed to encourage spending without player understanding.</p><p>The semi-pay-to-win nature combined with lack of transparency makes World of Tanks problematic. While not as predatory as pure pay-to-win games like MapleStory, the system undermines competitive integrity and respects neither player time nor money.</p><p>World of Tanks should be approached cautiously. Free-to-play is possible but requires accepting disadvantages from premium tank spenders. The monetization doesn't justify the compromised competitive experience, making alternative tank games (like War Thunder) potentially better choices.</p>`,
