    comparable_slugs: ["world-of-tanks", "world-of-warships", "crossfire"],
    overview_html: `<p><strong>War Thunder</strong> features gacha-style mechanics through randomized vehicle and cosmetic containers costing 2.99 USD per pull. Unlike transparent cosmetic boxes, War Thunder's containers deliberately obscure contents—players don't know whether they're opening cosmetics or premium vehicles until the box is purchased. The system contains progression vehicles that provide gameplay advantages, creating pay-to-progress mechanics disguised as cosmetic gambling.</p><p>The monetization is particularly predatory because vehicle containers affect gameplay significantly. Premium vehicles earn more currency and accelerate progression compared to free vehicles, making spending not just convenient but competitively advantageous. The system deliberately obscures which containers contain cosmetics versus vehicles, maximizing confusion.</p>`,
    pity_explanation_html: `<p><strong>War Thunder has no pity system and deliberately hides container contents until purchase.</strong> Players cannot determine what they're purchasing before spending, making the system pure gambling without any transparency. The mixing of cosmetics and progression vehicles in hidden containers is designed to maximize spending confusion.</p><p>This is among the most predatory transparency approaches in gaming—players literally cannot make informed spending decisions because contents are hidden until purchase is complete.</p>`,
    history_html: `<p><strong>2012:</strong> War Thunder launches with minimal cosmetic monetization. Early systems are relatively transparent in function.</p><p><strong>2015-2018:</strong> Loot box systems gradually introduced with increasing complexity. Container contents become progressively less transparent.</p><p><strong>2019-2024:</strong> War Thunder's containers feature hidden contents that don't display until purchase is complete. This becomes the defining feature of the game's predatory monetization.</p>`,
    controversy_html: `<p>War Thunder is notorious for hidden container contents—players cannot see what cosmetics or vehicles are in containers before purchasing, making informed spending decisions impossible. This is deliberately predatory design that treats players as gambling targets.</p><p>The game has been investigated in multiple regions for predatory mechanics targeting younger audiences. The hidden container system is particularly problematic because it eliminates any possibility of informed consumer decision-making.</p>`,
    tips_html: `<p><strong>Tip 1:</strong> Never open containers in War Thunder—the hidden contents system is designed for maximum predation. Budget strictly if opening is unavoidable.</p><p><strong>Tip 2:</strong> War Thunder's monetization is among the most predatory in gaming. Consider whether the game is worth supporting with spending.</p><p><strong>Tip 3:</strong> Research community content to understand which vehicles provide competitive advantages through spending. Accept the pay-to-progress imbalance.</p><p><strong>Tip 4:</strong> Play War Thunder free-to-play if at all, accepting disadvantages from premium spenders. Never invest hope in progression matching paid players.</p><p><strong>Tip 5:</strong> Support regulatory efforts against hidden loot box contents. Gaijin Entertainment's hidden container system should be illegal.</p>`,
    editorial_html: `<p>War Thunder represents the absolute worst-case scenario for loot box transparency—hidden container contents that reveal only after purchase make informed spending decisions impossible. Players literally cannot determine whether they're buying cosmetics or premium vehicles because the system deliberately hides this information.</p><p>The combination of hidden contents, progression vehicles in loot boxes, and gacha-style randomization creates a system designed exclusively to maximize spending without consumer understanding. This should be illegal in developed markets and violates basic consumer protection principles.</p><p>War Thunder should be avoided for any spending. The game's monetization is predatory to an extent that approaches gambling for minors. If you play, do so free-to-play and accept the pay-to-progress imbalance as unavoidable. Gaijin Entertainment's monetization is among the most unethical in all gaming.</p>`,
